## Separate repair reserve

The user authorized the separate 15-minute repair after initial freeze. Repair began at 2026-09-16 21:14:27 UTC. The original 15-file submission, executable and feedback were retained in `repair/pre-repair-submission.tar.gz`; source was not rewritten.

Feedback contains one family, `injected_failures`, with an otherwise unexplained JSONDecodeError. A candidate-owned test-only interposer reproduced the exact exception on the original executable by returning a RocksDB admission write error: the socket response was empty. The failure artifacts remain at `tmp/failure-admission-submit-c5dlrx6e`. This is a matching reproduction; the evaluator's precise injection sequence remains unknown.

Production correction: `server.go` now sends a correlated `execution_failure` response on request-associated storage errors before the existing fatal stop. It does not return acceptance, a sequence, or a terminal receipt for the failed transition. No changes to storage policy, WAL/sync, schema, arena ownership, native ABI or scheduling were made.

Six focused injection cases passed after the fix: admission before/after native write, arena failure during evaluate/accepted work, terminal write error and cancellation write error. The complete normal and race suites then passed, including all existing unit/public/socket/crash checks and all six new fault cases. Completion of validation and hashes was recorded at 21:18:37 UTC (4 minutes 10 seconds after repair start); handoff finalization followed. No second evaluator result is available.

`tests/faults.c` is linked only into a test shared object; successful calls forward to the supplied adapter and designated RocksDB. It provides no barriers or scheduler hooks. The injected-after-write case checks that an error can leave complete new state without receiving an acceptance; restart remains authoritative. Independent database inspection follows writer exit in all cases. Test-only source and failure notes are retained, and `test.sh` includes the new regression in both normal and race modes.

